Section 1:  Something's missing from CS

The context is software bugs that are, at their heart, type errors.  For 70
years, we have treated physical quantities as "naked floats."  When
programming, we strip the metadata (the units) and leave a raw number.  I'd
like to argue this is a deep problem with types, something ever CS person
should resonate with.

Mike and I are building something we're calling the Noether REPL.  The REPL
part means it's used within the python read-evaluate-print loop, which native
python supplies so you can try it out and experiment with the language's
features, much like using a calculator (one common use case).  The Noether
part honors Emmy Noether, a mathematician who more than a century ago stated a
theorem much loved by physicists that relates symmetries to invariants.  I
won't go into details, as it's couched in the language of the calculus of
variations in Lagrangian mechanics, but it's deep and powerful.  

We attached the name Noether to it because, in a nutshell, the number and its
unit combine to make an invariant.  While the purist will argue that a free
abelian group is a better model (and even it's not quite correct), I'd
maintain that engineers will do fine with a vector space model.  A unit like
the newton, kg*m/s^2 in base SI units, is a "vector" in the sense of its base
dimensional units mass M, length L, and time T.  The dimensions of a newton
are often written M*L*T⁻².  In the context of Noether, this invariant doesn't
change with scaling transformations, which means you make a proportional
change in the length of the particular axis.  Thus, one kg of mass is scaled
to 2.2 pounds mass.  The overall Noether invariant "22.7 N" has two critically
needed parts:  the number and the units; the units are like the basis vectors
in a vector space:  if you change the basis vectors, the vector's components
change appropriately.  "22.7 N" is the expression as a particular
representation of this invariant, in the same fashion that a 3x3 matrix can
represent members of the abstract group SO(3), the special orthogonal group.
Transformations and invariants are core ideas in many fields.

In CS, the concept of type is fundamental.  Personally, I like to think about
it as an abstract thing like a group, field, or whatever structure works, that
captures the essential nature of the problem.  We naturally do this with
classes, unions, etc. in multiple programming languages -- and the
compilers/interpreters treat those types with reverent respect.  We should
want them to treat the units of a problem with the same respect and care.

In a simple example, the following working python code demonstrates why this
Noether invariant is an important and powerful idea.  

    def DogsAndCats(error=True):
        '''Each dog gets 0.2 kg of food and cats get 0.1 kg of the same food.
        We have to feed 7 dogs and 12 cats.  How much food do we need?
        '''
        from number import Num
        # Define semantic units
        x = Num("1 m")
        breakpoint() # ∞∞ 
        x.base("dog")         # Create a new semantic unit
        x.base("cat")
        # Define the input quantities
        num_dogs = Num("7 dog")
        num_cats = Num("12 cat")
        dog_food_rate = Num("0.2 kg/dog")
        cat_food_rate = Num("0.1 kg/cat")
        # Solve the problem
        if no_programming_error:
            food_mass = num_dogs*dog_food_rate + num_cats*cat_food_rate
        else:
            food_mass = num_cats*dog_food_rate + num_dogs*cat_food_rate
        # Print results
        print(f"number of dogs = {num_dogs}")
        print(f"number of cats = {num_cats}")
        print(f"dog food rate  = {dog_food_rate}")
        print(f"cat food rate  = {cat_food_rate}")
        print(f"amount of food = {food_mass}")

    if __name__ == "__main__":  
        import sys
        if len(sys.argv) > 1:
            DogsAndCats(False)
        else:
            DogsAndCats(True)

With error=False, you get

    number of dogs = 7 dog
    number of cats = 12 cat
    dog food rate  = 0.200 kg/dog
    cat food rate  = 0.100 kg/cat
    amount of food = 2.60 kg

but the variables are color-coded as to type.  With error=True, you get

    !…/plib> p a.py x
    Traceback (most recent call last):
      File "/gh/plib/a.py", line 30, in <module>
        DogsAndCats(False)
      File "/gh/plib/a.py", line 19, in DogsAndCats
        food_mass = num_cats*dog_food_rate + num_dogs*cat_food_rate
                    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      File "/gh/plib/number.py", line 223, in __add__
        adj_other = self._normalize(other, "add")
                    ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
      File "/gh/plib/number.py", line 757, in _normalize
        raise ValueError(f"Unit Mismatch: {factor_str}")
    ValueError: Unit Mismatch: conformability error

and most of can read the traceback and see both the offending line and what
the error is:  multiplying the number of cats by a mass rate for dogs.  That's
the power of "semantic units" at work.

We're calling these important strings "semantic units" because they capture
key information about the problem that has been missing from essentially all
the compilers and interpreters.  While there are add-on unit libraries in the
various langμAges, I'd like to make the case that this should be built into
the computing engine at the core level.  It should be part of every
calculation.  A famous units failure was the 1999 Mars Climate Orbiter which
crashed and was a loss of about half a billion dollars in 2026 money.  An
older units failure was told by doctor friends of mine about the confounding
of "gr" in prescriptions, which could be interpreted as either grams or
grains, leading to a 15 times under-dosage or over-dosage, neither of benefit
to the patient.  Mike made an important observation:  this isn't just for
physics; an ERP system could use this to prevent a million-dollar
reconciliation error by ensuring "Unit_Price" and "Quantity" don't get
swapped.

