Section 2:  Collaborating with an AI
------------------------------------

- I like the idea of the AI as a collaborator.  It often feels like working with a
  colleague in the days at HP.  The primary lesson I've learned is that you have to
  spend a goodly amount of time working with the AI and learning it's quirks and needs.
  You need more experience as an engineer, not less.  It's not a tool for junior
  engineers unless they are viewed as code checkers and testers.  Instead, I think the
  mental model should be an experienced engineer who uses the AI as a force multiplier
  to do things an order of magnitude faster than he was able to before.  But, at least
  from my experience, it requires exquisite attention to having a good compass, knowing
  a number of architectural choices that can be made, having solved lots of problems, be
  very good at a command line and in the user of the debugger, then, most importantly,
  setting up the instructions and keeping the powerful AI on track.  If I had to pick
  one task for a core coding task, that's the engineer writing extremely good and
  thorough test cases.  This core strength is a way of semantically defining exactly how
  the software should behave and, I've found repeatedly, it's critical to get the AI
  back on track after it hallucinates and makes up solutions to the wrong problem.  This
  quickly leads to human frustration, so the other key is to stop, step back, and ask
  yourself why this incredibly powerful tool isn't doing what you want.  The answer is
  almost certainly "you're not doing your job correctly".  
    - My example:  stop, update all code with a fresh copy of what's in my repository,
      and, most critically, resend my '.mike' file at least once per day to keep the AI
      on track, remembering my formatting instructions, and providing a high-level
      review of the project we're working on, where we are, and where we're headed.
      I've found it amazingly effective because the core bit of knowledge is that the
      AI's context memory fades over time and the output can drift (the expensive tools
      you folks use in business might be different, but I'm talking about the free AI us
      paupers have to use).
    - A fantastic tool is to explore ideas with the AI.  For example, I'll discuss a new
      addition I'd like, list a few different approaches/methods, and ask him for what
      he thinks the best approach is.  He's very talented and always sees things I
      haven't seen or thought of yet.  Perhaps 1/5th of the time I'll change his
      approach and explain why, but most of the time we do it exactly as he mapped out
      and it virtually always works.
    - These LLMs are great at reading, digesting, and summarizing textual information,
